1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,047
<font color="#000000">This paper, Mechanistic Mode Connectivity,</font>

2
00:00:03,127 --> 00:00:07,862
<font color="#000000">studies neural network loss landscapes through the
lens of mode connectivity.</font>

3
00:00:07,942 --> 00:00:13,554
<font color="#000000">The authors ask whether minimizers that rely on
different mechanisms for making predictions</font>

4
00:00:13,634 --> 00:00:16,243
<font color="#000000">are connected via simple paths of low loss.</font>

5
00:00:16,323 --> 00:00:21,371
<font color="#000000">They define mechanistic similarity as shared
invariances to input transformations,</font>

6
00:00:21,451 --> 00:00:27,751
<font color="#000000">and show that a lack of linear connectivity between
two models implies they use dissimilar mechanisms.</font>

7
00:00:27,831 --> 00:00:32,128
<font color="#000000">Building on this insight, they propose
connectivity-based fine-tuning,</font>

8
00:00:32,208 --> 00:00:38,320
<font color="#000000">a method to deliberately alter a model's mechanisms
and reduce its reliance on spurious attributes.</font>

9
00:00:38,700 --> 00:00:42,987
<font color="#000000">Modern deep networks have infinitely many global
minimizers,</font>

10
00:00:43,067 --> 00:00:47,355
<font color="#000000">and the mode-connectivity literature shows these
minimizers are joined by surprisingly simple,</font>

11
00:00:47,435 --> 00:00:51,722
<font color="#000000">low-loss paths.</font>

12
00:00:51,802 --> 00:00:56,090
<font color="#000000">But this literature has ignored a crucial question:
what mechanisms do the connected models actually use?</font>

13
00:00:56,170 --> 00:01:00,457
<font color="#000000">Two models can both reach low loss while relying on
entirely different attributes of the input,</font>

14
00:01:00,537 --> 00:01:04,825
<font color="#000000">such as an object's background versus its shape.</font>

15
00:01:04,905 --> 00:01:09,192
<font color="#000000">The paper argues we cannot understand loss
landscapes, or safely fine-tune models,</font>

16
00:01:09,273 --> 00:01:13,560
<font color="#000000">without accounting for these prediction mechanisms.</font>

17
00:01:13,940 --> 00:01:15,519
<font color="#000000">Why does this matter now?</font>

18
00:01:15,599 --> 00:01:20,365
<font color="#000000">In practice we constantly fine-tune pretrained
models on downstream data,</font>

19
00:01:20,445 --> 00:01:23,086
<font color="#000000">assuming this fixes undesirable behavior.</font>

20
00:01:23,166 --> 00:01:29,790
<font color="#000000">But prior work shows fine-tuned models often remain
linearly connected to their pretraining solution.</font>

21
00:01:29,870 --> 00:01:33,241
<font color="#000000">If linear connectivity is tied to shared mechanisms,</font>

22
00:01:33,321 --> 00:01:39,348
<font color="#000000">then naive fine-tuning on clean data might never
remove a model's reliance on spurious cues.</font>

23
00:01:39,428 --> 00:01:45,122
<font color="#000000">Understanding the relationship between connectivity
and mechanism is therefore directly</font>

24
00:01:45,202 --> 00:01:48,640
<font color="#000000">relevant to robustness and safe adaptation of models.</font>

25
00:01:49,020 --> 00:01:52,822
<font color="#000000">The paper makes three main contributions.</font>

26
00:01:52,902 --> 00:01:56,704
<font color="#000000">First,</font>

27
00:01:56,784 --> 00:02:00,587
<font color="#000000">it defines mechanistic similarity: two models are
mechanistically similar if they are</font>

28
00:02:00,667 --> 00:02:04,469
<font color="#000000">invariant to the same set of unit interventions on
the data-generating process.</font>

29
00:02:04,549 --> 00:02:08,351
<font color="#000000">Second, it characterizes connectivity,</font>

30
00:02:08,431 --> 00:02:12,233
<font color="#000000">proving that if two models lack linear connectivity
up to architectural symmetries,</font>

31
00:02:12,313 --> 00:02:16,116
<font color="#000000">they must be mechanistically dissimilar.</font>

32
00:02:16,196 --> 00:02:19,998
<font color="#000000">Third, motivated by this result, it proposes
Connectivity-Based Fine-Tuning,</font>

33
00:02:20,078 --> 00:02:23,880
<font color="#000000">a method that exploits loss barriers to steer a
model toward the mechanisms we actually want.</font>

34
00:02:24,260 --> 00:02:29,564
<font color="#000000">The method rests on defining mechanistic similarity
through invariance to interventions</font>

35
00:02:29,644 --> 00:02:31,483
<font color="#000000">on the data-generating process.</font>

36
00:02:31,563 --> 00:02:37,115
<font color="#000000">The authors then prove that a loss barrier along
the linear path between two models implies</font>

37
00:02:37,195 --> 00:02:39,714
<font color="#000000">the models are mechanistically dissimilar,</font>

38
00:02:39,794 --> 00:02:42,190
<font color="#000000">they have learned different invariances.</font>

39
00:02:42,270 --> 00:02:45,903
<font color="#000000">Connectivity-Based Fine-Tuning turns this into an
algorithm.</font>

40
00:02:45,983 --> 00:02:50,545
<font color="#000000">It minimizes three terms: a cross-entropy loss on a
small cue-free dataset,</font>

41
00:02:50,625 --> 00:02:57,724
<font color="#000000">a barrier loss that deliberately raises loss along
the linear path to the cue-relying solution up to an upper bound,</font>

42
00:02:57,804 --> 00:03:04,779
<font color="#000000">and an invariance loss that aligns
penultimate-layer representations across counterfactual versions of each class.</font>

43
00:03:04,859 --> 00:03:08,617
<font color="#000000">Together these push the model to a mechanistically
dissimilar,</font>

44
00:03:08,697 --> 00:03:10,040
<font color="#000000">cue-invariant solution.</font>

45
00:03:10,420 --> 00:03:12,794
<font color="#000000">To measure mechanisms quantitatively,</font>

46
00:03:12,874 --> 00:03:19,890
<font color="#000000">the authors build synthetic datasets by embedding
easily separable spurious cues into standard vision data.</font>

47
00:03:19,970 --> 00:03:23,802
<font color="#000000">CIFAR-10 gets small box cues placed according to
the label,</font>

48
00:03:23,882 --> 00:03:27,715
<font color="#000000">CIFAR-100 gets cues colored and positioned by label
digits,</font>

49
00:03:27,795 --> 00:03:33,551
<font color="#000000">and the Dominoes dataset stacks CIFAR-10 images
with class-matched Fashion-MNIST images.</font>

50
00:03:33,631 --> 00:03:38,392
<font color="#000000">They vary the fraction of cued samples from sixty
to one hundred percent,</font>

51
00:03:38,472 --> 00:03:43,830
<font color="#000000">and pair every dataset with counterfactual test
sets that remove, keep, randomize,</font>

52
00:03:43,910 --> 00:03:45,157
<font color="#000000">or scramble the cue.</font>

53
00:03:45,237 --> 00:03:52,120
<font color="#000000">These counterfactuals reveal exactly how much a
model leans on the spurious cue versus the natural image.</font>

54
00:03:52,500 --> 00:03:55,754
<font color="#000000">The central empirical finding validates the theory.</font>

55
00:03:55,834 --> 00:04:00,003
<font color="#000000">When ResNet-18 models are trained with and without
spurious cues,</font>

56
00:04:00,083 --> 00:04:02,356
<font color="#000000">they are mechanistically dissimilar,</font>

57
00:04:02,436 --> 00:04:06,409
<font color="#000000">and the paper shows they cannot be connected by a
linear path,</font>

58
00:04:06,489 --> 00:04:09,612
<font color="#000000">even after accounting for permutation symmetries.</font>

59
00:04:09,692 --> 00:04:12,881
<font color="#000000">However, a quadratic path connects them with ease.</font>

60
00:04:12,961 --> 00:04:18,437
<font color="#000000">This confirms that linear disconnection is a
reliable signal of differing mechanisms.</font>

61
00:04:18,517 --> 00:04:24,124
<font color="#000000">It also explains why naive fine-tuning fails:
fine-tuned models stay linearly connected</font>

62
00:04:24,204 --> 00:04:29,550
<font color="#000000">to their pretraining solution and therefore keep
relying on the same spurious cues.</font>

63
00:04:29,930 --> 00:04:34,086
<font color="#000000">The authors ablate the two auxiliary terms in the
CBFT objective.</font>

64
00:04:34,166 --> 00:04:40,473
<font color="#000000">Removing the barrier loss prevents the model from
moving to a mechanistically dissimilar solution,</font>

65
00:04:40,553 --> 00:04:46,338
<font color="#000000">while removing the invariance loss means the model
no longer reliably selects the specific</font>

66
00:04:46,418 --> 00:04:48,880
<font color="#000000">cue-invariant mechanism the user wants.</font>

67
00:04:48,960 --> 00:04:52,203
<font color="#000000">In other words, the barrier loss handles the where,</font>

68
00:04:52,283 --> 00:04:55,071
<font color="#000000">pushing away from the cue-relying minimizer,</font>

69
00:04:55,151 --> 00:05:00,219
<font color="#000000">and the invariance loss handles the which, locking
onto the desired invariance.</font>

70
00:05:00,299 --> 00:05:02,370
<font color="#000000">Both are needed for CBFT to work.</font>

71
00:05:02,750 --> 00:05:05,039
<font color="#000000">The numbers make the effect concrete.</font>

72
00:05:05,119 --> 00:05:07,664
<font color="#000000">On CIFAR-10 with sixty percent cued data,</font>

73
00:05:07,744 --> 00:05:13,169
<font color="#000000">Connectivity-Based Fine-Tuning achieves
seventy-four percent accuracy without the cue,</font>

74
00:05:13,249 --> 00:05:15,282
<font color="#000000">seventy-two percent with the cue,</font>

75
00:05:15,362 --> 00:05:18,675
<font color="#000000">and seventy-three percent when the cue is randomized,</font>

76
00:05:18,755 --> 00:05:20,788
<font color="#000000">essentially invariant to the cue.</font>

77
00:05:20,868 --> 00:05:23,668
<font color="#000000">A standard fine-tuning baseline, by contrast,</font>

78
00:05:23,748 --> 00:05:30,134
<font color="#000000">hits ninety-nine percent with the cue but drops to
just seventeen percent when the cue is randomized,</font>

79
00:05:30,214 --> 00:05:33,271
<font color="#000000">revealing heavy reliance on the spurious feature.</font>

80
00:05:33,351 --> 00:05:37,625
<font color="#000000">And when the underlying image is randomized, CBFT
falls near chance,</font>

81
00:05:37,705 --> 00:05:42,170
<font color="#000000">around nine percent, showing it no longer predicts
from the cue at all.</font>

82
00:05:42,550 --> 00:05:46,815
<font color="#000000">The takeaway is that mode connectivity carries
mechanistic meaning.</font>

83
00:05:46,895 --> 00:05:52,328
<font color="#000000">If two low-loss models are not linearly connected,
they rely on different mechanisms,</font>

84
00:05:52,408 --> 00:05:54,922
<font color="#000000">and this fact can be turned into a tool.</font>

85
00:05:55,002 --> 00:06:00,564
<font color="#000000">Connectivity-Based Fine-Tuning uses it to
deliberately reshape what a model attends to,</font>

86
00:06:00,644 --> 00:06:05,039
<font color="#000000">removing reliance on spurious cues where ordinary
fine-tuning cannot.</font>

87
00:06:05,119 --> 00:06:06,855
<font color="#000000">Mode connectivity, in short,</font>

88
00:06:06,935 --> 00:06:13,600
<font color="#000000">is not merely a geometric curiosity but a practical
handle for building more robust and editable models.</font>
